  "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)=5x-4{x}^{2}.$ The definition of ${y}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=5x-4{x}^{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{(5(x+h)-4(x+h)^{2})-(5x-4{x}^{2})}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{5x+5h-4{x}^{2}-8xh-4{h}^{2}-5x+4{x}^{2}}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{h(5-8x-4h)}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(5-8x-4h) \\\\  & =5-8x-4(0) \\\\  & =5-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
